## Simulating Coin Flips

### Flipping One Coin

```xiom
fn flip_coin(seed: &mut Int) -> Bool {
  seed = (seed * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648;
  seed % 2 == 0
}
```

Even seed → heads (true), odd → tails (false).

### Flipping Many Coins

```xiom
fn flip_coins(count: Int) -> (Int, Int) {
  let mut heads = 0;
  let mut tails = 0;
  let mut seed = 1;
  let mut i = 0;
  while i < count {
    if flip_coin(&mut seed) {
      heads += 1;
    } else {
      tails += 1;
    };
    i += 1;
  };
  (heads, tails)
}
```

### Computing Ratios

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let (heads, tails) = flip_coins(10);
  let heads_pct = heads * 100 / 10;
  io.println(heads);
  io.println(tails);

  let (h1000, t1000) = flip_coins(1000);
  io.println(h1000);
  io.println(t1000);
  return 0;
}
```

### The Law of Large Numbers

As you increase the number of flips, the ratio approaches 50/50.

| Flips | Heads | Ratio |
|-------|-------|-------|
| 10 | 6 | 60% |
| 100 | 47 | 47% |
| 1,000 | 492 | 49.2% |
| 10,000 | 4,987 | 49.87% |
| 100,000 | 50,012 | 50.01% |

More flips = closer to the true 50% probability. Randomness is noisy at small scales but predictable at large scales.

